1
00:00:00,510 --> 00:00:03,540
Buffalo, New York, is famous for snow.

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:08,060
For blizzards that bury cars and shut the city down for days.

3
00:00:08,130 --> 00:00:13,110
But the deadliest natural disaster in Buffalo's history didn't come with snow.

4
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:16,810
It came on an October night in 1844…

5
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:18,640
and it came out of the lake.

6
00:00:18,710 --> 00:00:20,993
Just before midnight on the eighteenth of October,

7
00:00:21,013 --> 00:00:23,940
the wind swung around and began to blow hard from the southwest,

8
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:25,690
straight down the length of Lake Erie.

9
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:32,090
And the lake came with it.

10
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:36,065
In less than half an hour, the whole lower part of the city,

11
00:00:36,085 --> 00:00:39,140
everything south of the canal, was under water.

12
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:41,540
Two feet deep in some streets.

13
00:00:41,610 --> 00:00:43,930
Eight feet in others.

14
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,310
People woke up with the lake inside their homes.

15
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:49,320
By the time it was over…

16
00:00:49,390 --> 00:00:52,660
78 people were dead.

17
00:00:52,730 --> 00:00:53,900
There was no hurricane.

18
00:00:53,970 --> 00:00:56,300
No dam broke.

19
00:00:56,370 --> 00:01:02,159
The lake had been pushed away to its far end, held there by the wind for days,

20
00:01:02,179 --> 00:01:03,350
and then let go.

21
00:01:03,850 --> 00:01:09,370
And the same lake did the same thing again, last winter.

22
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:13,310
To understand how a lake can do that, look at its shape.

23
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:20,360
Lake Erie is about 240 miles long, and it runs from southwest to northeast.

24
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:23,650
Toledo, Ohio, sits at one end.

25
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:25,920
Buffalo sits at the other.

26
00:01:25,990 --> 00:01:29,190
It's also the shallowest of the five Great Lakes.

27
00:01:29,260 --> 00:01:32,850
On average, it's only about 60 feet deep.

28
00:01:32,920 --> 00:01:37,830
Lake Superior, on average, is almost eight times deeper.

29
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:40,630
Shallow water is easy to push.

30
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:44,992
And Lake Erie's worst seiches come when strong winds blow from the southwest,

31
00:01:45,012 --> 00:01:47,680
right along the lake, from one end to the other.

32
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:55,600
The National Weather Service describes Lake Erie as acting like "a giant bathtub."

33
00:01:55,670 --> 00:01:58,626
When a strong wind blows along it for hours,

34
00:01:58,646 --> 00:02:02,210
the water piles up at one end and drops at the other.

35
00:02:02,280 --> 00:02:04,842
In an extreme case, the weather service says,

36
00:02:04,862 --> 00:02:09,260
the water at Toledo and the water at Buffalo can differ by more than 10 feet.

37
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:13,660
It works in both directions.

38
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:17,484
When strong winds blow from the east instead, it's the western shore's turn,

39
00:02:17,504 --> 00:02:20,710
and the weather service watches the water at Toledo for flooding.

40
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:28,470
And when the wind stops, or turns, the water doesn't just settle back.

41
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:30,520
It rocks.

42
00:02:30,590 --> 00:02:32,640
That rocking has a name.

43
00:02:32,710 --> 00:02:33,660
Seiche.

44
00:02:33,730 --> 00:02:39,356
It's spelled S, E, I, C, H, E.

45
00:02:39,426 --> 00:02:43,806
In 1844, Buffalo was one of the fastest-growing cities in America,

46
00:02:43,826 --> 00:02:46,406
and it owed almost everything to water.

47
00:02:48,026 --> 00:02:53,716
The Erie Canal had opened in 1825, and Buffalo was where it ended.

48
00:02:53,786 --> 00:02:57,516
Grain and timber came across the lakes by steamboat.

49
00:02:57,586 --> 00:03:02,089
At Buffalo, they were loaded onto canal boats and sent east,

50
00:03:02,109 --> 00:03:04,276
all the way to New York City.

51
00:03:04,346 --> 00:03:09,666
People came the other way, thousands of them, heading west across the lakes.

52
00:03:09,736 --> 00:03:13,586
That waterfront was crowded, busy, and low.

53
00:03:13,656 --> 00:03:16,963
South of the canal lay the lower part of the city: docks, warehouses, hotels,

54
00:03:16,983 --> 00:03:20,636
boarding houses, and small wooden homes where canal workers and their families lived.

55
00:03:25,986 --> 00:03:31,056
Across Buffalo Creek were more homes, on low, flat ground.

56
00:03:31,126 --> 00:03:35,186
Out in the harbor stood a seawall and a stone pier,

57
00:03:35,206 --> 00:03:38,066
built to keep the lake in its place.

58
00:03:38,136 --> 00:03:41,936
The trouble started with a wind that seemed harmless.

59
00:03:42,006 --> 00:03:47,156
For several days before the storm, a stiff wind had been blowing from the east.

60
00:03:47,226 --> 00:03:51,496
A Buffalo newspaper explained afterward what it had done.

61
00:03:51,566 --> 00:03:57,325
It "had driven the water up the lake," away from Buffalo, and toward the far,

62
00:03:57,345 --> 00:03:58,226
western end.

63
00:03:58,296 --> 00:04:00,256
Think of the bathtub again.

64
00:04:00,326 --> 00:04:01,756
The lake was being tilted.

65
00:04:01,826 --> 00:04:04,116
Lower at Buffalo's end.

66
00:04:04,186 --> 00:04:06,116
Higher at Toledo's.

67
00:04:06,186 --> 00:04:08,586
And the wind was holding it there.

68
00:04:08,656 --> 00:04:14,027
On the evening of Friday, October 18, four steamboats left Buffalo,

69
00:04:14,047 --> 00:04:15,636
heading up the lake.

70
00:04:15,956 --> 00:04:17,106
The St. Louis.

71
00:04:17,176 --> 00:04:18,576
The Robert Fulton.

72
00:04:18,646 --> 00:04:20,166
The Indian Queen.

73
00:04:20,236 --> 00:04:25,929
And the Julia Palmer, with about 400 passengers on board.

74
00:04:25,999 --> 00:04:29,099
Then, at about midnight, the wind changed.

75
00:04:29,169 --> 00:04:32,280
It swung around to the southwest, and it blew, a later history says,

76
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:36,149
"with a tremendous force never before or since known to the inhabitants of Buffalo."

77
00:04:40,909 --> 00:04:46,049
Now it was blowing straight down the lake, from Toledo toward Buffalo.

78
00:04:46,119 --> 00:04:50,375
All the water that had been held at the far end was released,

79
00:04:50,395 --> 00:04:52,479
and driven back the other way.

80
00:04:52,549 --> 00:04:54,749
The newspaper put it simply.

81
00:04:54,819 --> 00:04:59,249
The water "came down before it as if a dam had broken away."

82
00:04:59,319 --> 00:05:01,681
It ran down 240 miles of lake,

83
00:05:01,701 --> 00:05:06,129
and it piled up at the one place it could go no further.

84
00:05:06,199 --> 00:05:07,559
The eastern end…

85
00:05:07,629 --> 00:05:09,879
Buffalo.

86
00:05:09,949 --> 00:05:13,099
It tore holes in the seawall and the stone pier,

87
00:05:13,119 --> 00:05:16,929
and carried stones weighing many tons ten and twenty feet.

88
00:05:17,959 --> 00:05:19,851
By the newspaper's measure,

89
00:05:19,871 --> 00:05:24,099
the lake rose 22 feet above where it had stood that evening.

90
00:05:24,169 --> 00:05:25,716
And in less than half an hour,

91
00:05:25,736 --> 00:05:29,373
"the whole lower part of the city south of the canal" was under water,

92
00:05:29,393 --> 00:05:31,149
"from two to eight feet in depth."

93
00:05:34,876 --> 00:05:36,156
It was midnight.

94
00:05:36,226 --> 00:05:38,946
Almost everyone in the lower city was asleep.

95
00:05:39,016 --> 00:05:44,336
A later history called it "an avalanche of waters upon a sleeping community."

96
00:05:44,406 --> 00:05:48,376
One man across the creek was woken by the noise of the wind.

97
00:05:48,446 --> 00:05:53,126
He guessed the water might rise, and ran to wake his family.

98
00:05:53,196 --> 00:05:55,680
Before he could get his trousers on,

99
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:59,366
the water in his house was more than three feet deep.

100
00:05:59,436 --> 00:06:05,526
On Main Street, near the bridge, a witness saw it come "in one huge wave."

101
00:06:05,596 --> 00:06:10,356
At the corner of Main and Ohio Streets, the water stood six feet deep.

102
00:06:10,426 --> 00:06:15,906
At Huff's Hotel, at Main and Scott, six feet.

103
00:06:15,976 --> 00:06:21,786
In basements and on ground floors, there was almost no time to get out.

104
00:06:21,856 --> 00:06:26,016
In one house, two families climbed to the second floor.

105
00:06:26,086 --> 00:06:29,226
The water followed them, and they climbed onto the roof.

106
00:06:29,296 --> 00:06:33,226
Then the flood lifted the whole house off its foundation…

107
00:06:33,296 --> 00:06:35,936
and floated it away.

108
00:06:36,006 --> 00:06:40,146
The roof split in two, with a family on each half.

109
00:06:40,216 --> 00:06:45,716
They drifted across the creek and over the flats, more than a mile in the dark.

110
00:06:45,786 --> 00:06:50,928
"The most fearful midnight voyaging," one account says, "that ever a man,

111
00:06:50,948 --> 00:06:52,766
wife and child underwent."

112
00:06:53,486 --> 00:06:57,516
Neighbors, risking their own lives, pulled them into the second story of a house,

113
00:06:57,536 --> 00:07:00,466
just before the rafts were swept into Little Buffalo Creek.

114
00:07:03,116 --> 00:07:07,019
Another family clung to their roof all the way to the Hydraulics,

115
00:07:07,039 --> 00:07:10,096
a mile and a half from where their house had stood.

116
00:07:10,747 --> 00:07:15,637
Out on the lake, the four steamboats met the gale at its height.

117
00:07:15,707 --> 00:07:18,697
Off Dunkirk, the St. Louis broke her shaft,

118
00:07:18,717 --> 00:07:22,687
and four of her passengers were swept overboard and lost.

119
00:07:22,797 --> 00:07:26,145
The Robert Fulton lost two or three people overboard,

120
00:07:26,165 --> 00:07:29,387
and was thrown up on the beach near Sturgeon Point.

121
00:07:29,457 --> 00:07:30,767
A total wreck.

122
00:07:30,837 --> 00:07:33,937
The Julia Palmer couldn't fight it at all.

123
00:07:34,007 --> 00:07:36,867
She was driven helplessly back down the lake.

124
00:07:36,937 --> 00:07:41,562
At about six in the morning, opposite the foot of Main Street in Buffalo,

125
00:07:41,582 --> 00:07:43,917
her anchors finally caught, and held.

126
00:07:45,907 --> 00:07:48,997
She rolled and pitched there all that day.

127
00:07:49,067 --> 00:07:51,299
Steamboats in 1844 burned wood,

128
00:07:51,319 --> 00:07:55,587
and the Julia Palmer had been fighting the storm for hours.

129
00:07:55,657 --> 00:08:01,297
Then a horse swam ashore from her, with a letter tied to its mane.

130
00:08:01,367 --> 00:08:04,157
They had burned all their wood, it said.

131
00:08:04,227 --> 00:08:04,857
They were…

132
00:08:04,927 --> 00:08:08,067
"now burning the furniture."

133
00:08:08,137 --> 00:08:11,471
On Sunday morning, when the water had gone down,

134
00:08:11,491 --> 00:08:14,127
the Julia Palmer came into the harbor.

135
00:08:14,197 --> 00:08:18,059
Everyone on board was safe.

136
00:08:18,129 --> 00:08:21,909
In the morning, Buffalo saw what the lake had done.

137
00:08:21,979 --> 00:08:26,939
Canal boats lay out in the streets, on Division, Eagle, and Clinton.

138
00:08:27,009 --> 00:08:33,279
A steamboat, the Columbus, sat in a pasture 200 feet from the creek.

139
00:08:33,349 --> 00:08:36,793
More than 80 canal boats had been driven ashore,

140
00:08:36,813 --> 00:08:39,969
and more than 200 small buildings were gone.

141
00:08:40,039 --> 00:08:42,784
Across the creek, the newspaper said,

142
00:08:42,804 --> 00:08:46,819
there was "scarcely a house in its original position."

143
00:08:46,889 --> 00:08:51,679
Most of the survivors had escaped in their nightclothes, with nothing else.

144
00:08:51,749 --> 00:08:56,229
The city opened the basement of the Mohawk Market as a shelter,

145
00:08:56,249 --> 00:08:58,729
and asked for clothing of any kind.

146
00:08:59,089 --> 00:09:00,789
Then came the count.

147
00:09:00,859 --> 00:09:05,319
The newspapers said it was impossible to get "at anything like accuracy."

148
00:09:05,389 --> 00:09:10,219
Their first list had 39 dead, "besides several who are missing."

149
00:09:10,289 --> 00:09:16,329
Later records put the toll at 53 in Buffalo, and 25 on the lake.

150
00:09:16,399 --> 00:09:19,479
78 in all.

151
00:09:19,549 --> 00:09:19,925
Today,

152
00:09:19,945 --> 00:09:26,199
New York Sea Grant still calls it "the most destructive documented seiche in Western New York."

153
00:09:26,269 --> 00:09:31,029
Local historians call it the worst natural disaster in Buffalo's history.

154
00:09:31,099 --> 00:09:35,502
And yet most people in Buffalo have never heard of it.

155
00:09:35,572 --> 00:09:41,242
If you watched the last video on this channel, you've heard the word seiche before.

156
00:09:41,312 --> 00:09:47,932
In 1954, a killer wave hit Chicago, and for decades it was called a seiche.

157
00:09:48,002 --> 00:09:48,922
It wasn't.

158
00:09:48,992 --> 00:09:53,392
It was a meteotsunami: one fast wave, over in minutes.

159
00:09:53,462 --> 00:09:57,642
What hit Buffalo in 1844 was the real thing.

160
00:09:57,712 --> 00:10:00,872
A seiche isn't one wave.

161
00:10:00,942 --> 00:10:04,978
It's the whole lake tilting, and then rocking back and forth, end to end,

162
00:10:04,998 --> 00:10:07,922
the way water rocks in a bathtub after you climb out.

163
00:10:10,772 --> 00:10:16,078
On Lake Erie, one full rock, from one end to the other and back again,

164
00:10:16,098 --> 00:10:17,752
takes around 14 hours.

165
00:10:19,082 --> 00:10:21,342
That's what makes a seiche so dangerous.

166
00:10:21,412 --> 00:10:23,182
It's slow.

167
00:10:23,252 --> 00:10:27,262
It can keep going long after the wind that started it.

168
00:10:27,332 --> 00:10:31,372
And the water that leaves one end of the lake hasn't gone anywhere.

169
00:10:31,442 --> 00:10:33,552
It's piled up at the other end…

170
00:10:33,622 --> 00:10:36,645
waiting to come back.

171
00:10:36,715 --> 00:10:41,455
So why does almost nobody remember the worst night in Buffalo's history?

172
00:10:41,525 --> 00:10:43,715
Part of the answer may be pictures.

173
00:10:43,785 --> 00:10:47,242
Photography had been invented only five years earlier,

174
00:10:47,262 --> 00:10:50,655
and there are no known pictures of that night at all.

175
00:10:50,725 --> 00:10:51,765
Not a photograph.

176
00:10:51,835 --> 00:10:53,355
Not even a drawing.

177
00:10:53,425 --> 00:10:55,615
Only newspaper columns.

178
00:10:55,685 --> 00:10:57,225
Part of it is snow.

179
00:10:57,295 --> 00:10:59,885
Buffalo became the city of blizzards.

180
00:10:59,955 --> 00:11:03,045
Those are the storms people tell stories about.

181
00:11:03,115 --> 00:11:06,145
And part of it is that Buffalo built its way out.

182
00:11:06,215 --> 00:11:13,015
In the 1860s, work began on a long breakwater out in the lake to shelter the harbor.

183
00:11:13,085 --> 00:11:16,279
Buffalo still gets two or three seiches a year,

184
00:11:16,299 --> 00:11:19,355
but the breakwater takes most of their force.

185
00:11:19,425 --> 00:11:23,425
The old canal district was rebuilt, and rebuilt again.

186
00:11:23,495 --> 00:11:29,255
Today, the site of the old canal harbor is a waterfront district called Canalside.

187
00:11:29,325 --> 00:11:30,745
The city changed…

188
00:11:30,815 --> 00:11:33,598
The lake didn't.

189
00:11:33,668 --> 00:11:40,648
On November 15, 2020, storm-force winds blew down Lake Erie from the southwest.

190
00:11:41,038 --> 00:11:44,602
For the first time since the remains of Hurricane Sandy in 2012,

191
00:11:44,622 --> 00:11:48,018
the weather service put the whole lake under a storm warning.

192
00:11:50,178 --> 00:11:56,138
It measured a difference of 14 feet between the water at Buffalo and the water at Toledo.

193
00:11:56,208 --> 00:11:57,856
Halfway along the lake, at Erie, Pennsylvania,

194
00:11:57,876 --> 00:12:01,012
the water climbed more than seven feet above its chart level and flooded the lakeshore,

195
00:12:01,032 --> 00:12:03,188
and several homes in Millcreek Township had to be evacuated.

196
00:12:09,878 --> 00:12:12,508
The bathtub had tilted again.

197
00:12:12,578 --> 00:12:14,133
Then, on December 29, 2025,

198
00:12:14,153 --> 00:12:17,983
a winter storm hit the lakes with gusts of 60 to 80 miles an hour,

199
00:12:18,003 --> 00:12:19,558
and set off another seiche.

200
00:12:23,758 --> 00:12:29,958
At the western end, the water drained away from the shore and left the lake bed bare.

201
00:12:30,028 --> 00:12:34,208
At the other end, the water rose against Buffalo.

202
00:12:34,278 --> 00:12:35,378
The same lake.

203
00:12:35,448 --> 00:12:36,978
The same wind.

204
00:12:37,048 --> 00:12:38,048
The same direction.

205
00:12:38,118 --> 00:12:43,241
A strong seiche like this comes every few years.

206
00:12:43,311 --> 00:12:46,951
There are two dangerous moments in a seiche.

207
00:12:47,021 --> 00:12:51,467
The first is the one Buffalo met in 1844: the water arriving, in the dark,

208
00:12:51,487 --> 00:12:54,001
faster than anyone can get out of its way.

209
00:12:56,601 --> 00:12:58,521
The second is quieter.

210
00:12:58,591 --> 00:13:01,121
It's the moment the lake pulls back.

211
00:13:01,191 --> 00:13:03,997
The water drains away, the lake bed is bare,

212
00:13:04,017 --> 00:13:08,171
and people are tempted to walk out and see what it's left behind.

213
00:13:09,101 --> 00:13:11,191
But that water isn't gone.

214
00:13:11,261 --> 00:13:16,841
It's piled up at the other end of the lake, and when the wind drops, it comes back.

215
00:13:16,911 --> 00:13:19,564
So if you ever see Lake Erie, or any lake,

216
00:13:19,584 --> 00:13:23,891
pull far back from the shore after a storm, don't walk out after it.

217
00:13:25,601 --> 00:13:30,881
And if the weather service warns of lakeshore flooding where you live, believe it.

218
00:13:30,951 --> 00:13:31,475
In 1844,

219
00:13:31,495 --> 00:13:37,931
the people of Buffalo went to sleep beside a lake that had been pushed away from them for days.

220
00:13:38,851 --> 00:13:40,311
Nothing told them…

221
00:13:40,381 --> 00:13:42,681
it was only waiting.

222
00:13:42,751 --> 00:13:45,947
78 people died in Buffalo's worst natural disaster,

223
00:13:45,967 --> 00:13:49,731
and the city famous for snow almost forgot it ever happened.

224
00:13:51,611 --> 00:13:55,541
If you know someone who lives on Lake Erie, send them this.

225
00:13:55,611 --> 00:13:59,391
Subscribe to open the next file.
